book.page:
{{w|Editor's note: an excerpt. The epigraphs are the author's own and are drawn from a longer work that has not survived.}}

{{c|"The desert does not advance. The desert is what remains when nothing opposes it."}}
{{c|— from the Salt Commentaries}}

The error of the freeholds is to fight the dune at its face. The face is where the dune is visible and therefore where it is weakest, and a season of labour at the face will move it a hand's breadth and cost you the season.

The dune is made at its back, where the wind lifts grain from the flat and lays it over the crest. A child with a hurdle-fence at the back of a dune does more in a month than a village at its face does in a year. This is not a technique. It is a way of seeing, and it is the whole of the discipline: find where the thing is being made, not where it is arriving.

{{c|"A people who plant for themselves will eat. A people who plant for their grandchildren will have grandchildren."}}
{{c|— from the Salt Commentaries}}

The vine is planted not for its fruit but for its root, and the root is not planted for the vine but for the soil, and the soil is not made for this generation. I have said this to freeholders and watched them do the arithmetic and reach the correct conclusion and plant nothing, because the correct conclusion is that they will not see it.

They are not wrong about the arithmetic. They are wrong about who is doing the counting.
